Assume `stdout` is a terminal (line-buffered) and `execlp` succeeds. What does this program print?

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <unistd.h>
int main(void) {
    printf("A\n");
    execlp("echo", "echo", "B", NULL);
    printf("C\n");
    return 0;
}
```

A 10 000-byte file is copied with the code below. Assume both `open` calls succeed, every `read` returns min(512, bytes remaining) and every `write` writes all `n` bytes. Ignore any system calls made during program start-up and exit. How many system calls does this fragment make in total?

```c
int in  = open("src", O_RDONLY);
int out = open("dst", O_WRONLY | O_CREAT | O_TRUNC, 0644);
char buf[512];
ssize_t n;
while ((n = read(in, buf, 512)) > 0)
    write(out, buf, n);
close(in);
close(out);
```

Assume `stdout` is a terminal (**line-buffered**), `fork` and `execlp` succeed, and `printf` output is not flushed except by a newline, a full buffer, or normal process exit.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/wait.h>
int main(void) {
    printf("hello ");
    if (fork() == 0) {
        execlp("echo", "echo", "child", NULL);
    } else {
        wait(NULL);
        printf("parent\n");
    }
    return 0;
}
```

