Assume `fork` and `execl` succeed, `/bin/echo` prints its argument followed by a newline, and output appears immediately. What is printed?

```c
int main(void) {
    pid_t p = fork();
    if (p == 0) {
        execl("/bin/echo", "echo", "X", NULL);
        printf("Y\n");
    } else {
        wait(NULL);
        printf("Z\n");
    }
    printf("W\n");
    return 0;
}
```

Consider the program below (assume all forks succeed). How many processes print the value **2**?

```c
int main(void) {
    int n = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
        if (fork() == 0)
            n++;
    }
    printf("%d\n", n);
    return 0;
}
```

Output is **unbuffered** (each `printf` appears immediately) and every `fork()` succeeds. How many lines in total (`A` lines plus `B` lines) are printed?

```c
int main(void) {
    for (int i = 0; i < 2; i++) {
        if (fork() && fork())
            printf("A\n");
        else
            fork();
    }
    printf("B\n");
    return 0;
}
```

Every `fork()` succeeds. What is the **sum** of all the values printed by all processes?

```c
int main(void) {
    int x = 1;
    for (int i = 1; i <= 3; i++) {
        if (fork() == 0)
            x = 3 * x;
        else
            x = x + i;
        if (x > 5)
            break;
    }
    printf("%d\n", x);
    return 0;
}
```

Consider this program:

```c
#include <signal.h>
#include <unistd.h>
static pid_t me;
static volatile int c = 0;
void h(int s) {
    if (++c == 1)
        kill(me, SIGUSR1);
    write(1, "h", 1);
}
int main(void) {
    struct sigaction sa = {0};   /* sa_mask empty, sa_flags = 0 */
    sa.sa_handler = h;
    sigaction(SIGUSR1, &sa, NULL);
    me = getpid();
    kill(me, SIGUSR1);
    write(1, "m", 1);
    _exit(0);
}
```

Assume: `sigaction`, `getpid`, `kill`, `write` and `_exit` are each exactly one system call; a pending, unblocked signal is delivered when the kernel is about to return to user mode, by returning to user mode **into the handler** instead of the interrupted code; while `h` runs, SIGUSR1 is blocked; when `h` returns, a trampoline makes one `sigreturn` system call that restores the saved context and signal mask; there are no other interrupts or faults. How many **mode switches** (user→kernel and kernel→user counted separately) occur from the first instruction of `main` until the process terminates?

`stdout` is redirected to a file with `./a.out > out`, so it is **fully buffered**. The stdio buffer holds **4096 bytes**; bytes are appended one at a time, and whenever the buffer becomes full its 4096 bytes are written with one `write()`. At normal termination (return from `main` / `exit()`), a non-empty buffer is written with one more `write()`; `_exit()` does **not** flush. `L` is a 100-character string (99 letters and a newline). All calls succeed.

```c
int main(void) {
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++) fputs(L, stdout);
    pid_t p = fork();
    for (int i = 0; i < 50; i++) fputs(L, stdout);
    if (p == 0) _exit(0);
    wait(NULL);
    return 0;
}
```

How many bytes does the file `out` contain when the parent has finished?

All calls succeed and `f` does not exist beforehand. What does file `f` contain after the parent finishes?

```c
int fd = open("f", O_RDWR | O_CREAT | O_TRUNC, 0644);
write(fd, "abcdef", 6);
if (fork() == 0) {
    int d = dup(fd);
    lseek(d, 1, SEEK_SET);
    write(fd, "XY", 2);
    int g = open("f", O_WRONLY | O_APPEND);
    write(g, "Q", 1);
    _exit(0);
}
wait(NULL);
write(fd, "Z", 1);
```
